WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:07.820
On I-10 when you go over the I-10 bridge, right on the left hand side, that was . . . All that was Olin.

2
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:14.580
And you'll probably see the production building is still there, and you might see something called Chemtura.

3
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:15.720
That was all part of us.

4
00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.040
It was called Biolab at that time.

5
00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:25.740
And then they have . . . We also produced something called hydrazine, which was used in the space industry.

6
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.160
It was a rocket fuel.

7
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.940
So that was part of our production stream, too.

8
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:35.500
But we had a lot of . . . lot of chemicals that we made.

9
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.000
And of course we had the bad, bad chemical, phosgene.

10
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.180
I don’t know If you've ever heard of that.

11
00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.920
We used that in our TDI [toluene diisocyanate] production.

12
00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.740
So we had a whole bunch of smaller plants that fueled the TDI plant.

13
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.540
It was the first chemical plant in the area.

14
00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:58.193
But then of course we got Conoco and PPG and all the other plants came in after this.

15
00:00:58.193 --> 00:01:02.860
Cities Service . . . But Olin was the first chemical plant in the area.

16
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.360
Like I said, in 1934.

17
00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.520
How did that happen?

18
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:06.960
How did it come to . . .?

19
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.200
You know I don't really know.

20
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.180
I think they . . . We were in a good location.

21
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.700
We had the water, where they could bring barges in.

22
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.340
We had the railroad.

23
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.400
We had the interstate.

24
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.980
So at that time it wasn't the interstate, but they had good highways.

25
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:31.080
And I think the thing that really sold them was probably the water.

26
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:39.420
Because they could bring ships and barges in to bring their produce in that they needed to . . . the raw materials in that they needed.

27
00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:44.320
But I knew that we were an ideal place, because it was the south, the warm weather.

28
00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:54.980
They didn't have to worry about freezing, chemicals freezing and plants freezing, pipes freezing and we . . . our natural resources.

29
00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:58.980
We had natural gas, electricity was good.

30
00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:04.880
So we had a lot of things that they needed, that made us look good for the chemical industry.

31
00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.360
And it thrived for many, many, many years.

32
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.320
But then in 2006, we shut down.

33
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:15.220
And like I said, we were union.

34
00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.320
The guys in the field were union.

35
00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.240
And when I first came into the plant, I was union.

36
00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.020
By the time it was shut down I'd become company . . . salaried.

37
00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:27.460
I belonged to the OPEIU [Office and Professional Employees International Union] which is a part of the AFL CIO.

38
00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:30.640
It was office and professionals union.

39
00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:39.680
And I was the first black to have a . . . become secretary or to become on the board.

40
00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.780
And I was able to go to a lot of the conventions that they had.

41
00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:49.700
Then Louisiana became a right to work state.

42
00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.340
And you couldn't get contracts.

43
00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:55.500
It was hard to get a contract negotiated.

44
00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.620
It was just awful.

45
00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:04.180
But when I first started working at Olin I was making three dollars and something an hour.

46
00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:07.700
It was in 1966, and I thought I was rich.

47
00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:11.200
But in the area, we were making more than the school teachers.

48
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.840
It drove our economy.

49
00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.440
And then in 1992, they put a cap on salaries.

50
00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:24.560
If you were coming in as a union employee you were kind of . . . There was a ceiling.

51
00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:28.240
You hit that ceiling and then you couldn’t move any further.

52
00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:29.680
You couldn't make any more money.

53
00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:37.460
So that was a bad thing, when they got the right to work in and started doing away with the unions.

54
00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:40.000
But while it lasted, it was really good.

55
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.260
People were able to make a good, good salary and feed our local economy.

56
00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.380
We were buying things.

57
00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:47.580
Buying homes.

58
00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:48.800
Buying cars.

59
00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:51.260
And nowadays I look at that and I think about that.

60
00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:56.920
I say if they didn't have that, I don't know what our area would be like.

61
00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:59.260
Because it would kind of be dead.

62
00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:07.900
If you didn't have the people working at the plants that was kind of driving the economy of the community.

63
00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:09.400
So we have so many plants now.

64
00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.100
We just have gobs of plants.

65
00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:11.820
Which is good.

66
00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:12.640
Which is a good thing.
